ABSTRACT = f"""Calling a second agent after a planning disruption can improve repair reliability, but its cost may exceed its value. This simulation study tests whether structural changes identify repairs worth auditing. A locally generated scheduling environment uses twelve-task instances, an exact feasibility validator and a bounded deterministic constraint-programming reference. Algorithmic repair and audit agents overlook constraint checks under explicit parametric error models; no language model supplies the reported outcomes. At the default slip rate, single-agent feasibility was {E.capture(feasA, 'build_manuscript.py:70: feasA', f'.3f', -1)} and audited feasibility was {E.capture(feasT, 'build_manuscript.py:70: feasT', f'.3f', -1)}, at {E.capture(tokT / tokA, 'build_manuscript.py:70: tokT / tokA', f'.2f', -1)} times the assumed token cost. The dependency-load feature had area under the receiver operating characteristic curve {E.capture(A_('load', 'eps', 0.02), "build_manuscript.py:70: A_('load', 'eps', 0.02)", f'.3f', -1)}. Thresholds are tuned on disjoint development instances, with cluster-level uncertainty and multiplicity correction for a fixed utility-comparison family. Added critical-path and resource-pressure proxies test specific structural alternatives. The largest selected gain across the exploratory price grid and error models was {E.capture(max_gain_all, 'build_manuscript.py:70: max_gain_all', f'.4f', -1)} utility units. The evidence does not support a useful dependency-change trigger under these assumptions. A validator-triggered audit can be preferable when validation is inexpensive. This negative result concerns the testbed and error models; it does not establish that structural routing fails for real language-model agents."""
blocks = []
A = blocks.append
A(('h1', '1. Introduction'))
A(('p', 'A second agent is the most common remedy when one language-model agent is not reliable enough. Reviewers, verifiers and debaters improve accuracy, but they also multiply tokens and latency. Failure analyses of multi-agent systems show that more agents do not remove failures and can add coordination errors of their own (Cemri et al., 2025), and a large controlled study found that the best architecture depends on how well coordination matches the structure of the task (Kim et al., 2026). The practical question is therefore not whether a second agent helps, but when it is worth its cost.'))
A(('p', "Recent studies address this question by choosing the collaboration structure from properties of the task: a graph network predicts which topology solves a code-generation problem at the lowest cost (Hong, 2026), and a prompt-based router chooses between typed graphs and natural language for each delegation (Banerjee & Chadha, 2026). Topology selection acts before the task; handoff format is selected before each delegation. Neither evaluates the specific post-disruption second-agent trigger tested here. In dynamic planning the decision can be made later, at the moment something changes. When a disruption touches only one task with slack, the original agent can patch the plan. When it rewires dependencies between several tasks, a mistake in one check can invalidate the whole schedule, and a second pair of eyes seems more valuable. This intuition motivates the hypothesis we test: *the amount of dependency change after a disruption predicts when a single agent's repair will be wrong, and calling a second agent only above a threshold of that signal is more efficient than calling it always, never, or at random with the same frequency.*"))
A(('p', 'The intuition is plausible and, as we show, only weakly supported by data from a controlled testbed. We report the study as a measurement of a decision signal, not as a method paper, and structure it around three questions:'))
A(('ol', ["RQ1: How well do features of the constraint difference predict that a single agent's repair will be infeasible?", 'RQ2: Does a trigger on those features beat always-single, always-team and a randomly mixed policy with the same invocation rate, under an explicit token-price utility?', 'RQ3: How does a failure-triggered rule that relies on an external validator compare, and at what validator cost does the comparison reverse?']))
A(('h1', '2. Literature Review'))
A(('h2', '2.1. Adaptive collaboration in multi-agent systems'))
A(('p', 'The difficulty-aware topology selector of Hong (2026) predicts the success probability of each topology for a code problem and picks the best predicted success minus a tunable cost penalty. At 40% of the cost of always using a hierarchical team it reports higher pass@1. Routed Graph Handoff selects, before each delegation, whether to send a typed dependency graph or free text, reporting large savings in tokens (Banerjee & Chadha, 2026). Both adapt collaboration to task content, but their decisions occur at different boundaries. Routed Graph Handoff uses a single prompt-based classifier rather than a learned routing model, and its limitations acknowledge coarse task-type routing and unresolved execution-time headroom. Our setting differs in two ways: the decision is taken after a disruption, and the available signal is the difference between the dependency graphs before and after it.'))
A(('h2', '2.2. Dynamic scheduling and rescheduling'))
A(('p', 'Rescheduling after disruptions is a mature topic in operations research (Vieira et al., 2003), and resource-constrained project scheduling is NP-hard in general (Blazewicz et al., 1983). REALM-Bench proposes fourteen adjustable scheduling challenges for evaluating language-model agents and teams under disruptions (Geng & Chang, 2025). We use instances that follow the same ideas, precedence constraints, shared resources and disruptions, but generate them ourselves so that a bounded reference solution can be computed with a constraint-programming solver (Perron & Furnon, 2025). The study is not run on REALM-Bench itself.'))
A(('h2', '2.3. Verification after failure'))
A(('p', 'Wrapping tool calls with postcondition checks reduces unnecessary executions while keeping task success (Mansoor et al., 2026). The same idea underlies a failure-triggered collaboration rule: act, validate, and call help only when validation fails. Such a rule needs a validator, which is not always available for open-ended work. Part of our contribution is to quantify the validator cost at which the rule stops being preferable to a rule based on the structure of the change.'))
A(('h1', '3. Materials and Methods'))
A(('h2', '3.1. Research design'))
A(('p', f"The study is a computational experiment with a development and a test split. Instances are generated from seeds; the development split ({E.capture(N_DEV_BASES, 'build_manuscript.py:87: N_DEV_BASES', '', -1)} base instances per family) is used only to tune the trigger threshold, and the test split ({E.capture(N_TEST_BASES, 'build_manuscript.py:87: N_TEST_BASES', '', -1)} base instances per family, disjoint seeds) is used for every reported number. Each base instance receives three disruptions, each repair is repeated {E.capture(REPS, 'build_manuscript.py:87: REPS', '', -1)} times with independent agent noise, and all policies are evaluated on the same episodes. Development base seeds start at 0, test seeds at 1000; the wide-family offset is 100000. No human participants or language-model calls are involved."))
A(('h2', '3.2. Scheduling instances and disruptions'))
A(('p', 'Each instance has 12 tasks with integer durations of 1 to 5, a precedence graph, and two renewable resources with task demands. The *pipeline* family is a four-layer graph with light resource use; the *wide* family has a shallow graph and tight resource contention. The baseline schedule is a feasible incumbent of a CP-SAT model (Perron & Furnon, 2025). Three disruptions are applied to the instance:'))
A(('ul', ['**Local:** the duration of a task with at most two transitive successors grows by one or two units.', '**Dependency:** two to four new precedence edges or mutual-exclusion pairs are added.', '**Cascade:** five to seven such constraints are added. This type, and the load-dependent error condition below, were added after a pilot showed small effects with the first two types, so they are exploratory extensions.']))
A(('p', 'The *affected region* is the set of tasks touched by the disruption plus all their descendants. A feasible repair is a schedule that satisfies every constraint of the disrupted instance, checked by an exact validator; the reference makespan is the CP-SAT incumbent for the disrupted instance.'))
A(('p', 'The reference solver uses one worker, a fixed random seed and a deterministic-time budget of 0.1 units per solve, with a serial feasible hint and resource/path lower bounds. Every result records solver status and lower bound; uncertified incumbents are never described as optima. A serial fallback retains hard seeds if no solver incumbent is available. Makespan deviations are measured relative to the incumbent and can be negative; they are not certified optimality gaps.'))
A(('h2', '3.3. Repair agent and audit agent'))
A(('p', 'The first agent re-places the tasks of the affected region in topological order. For each task it performs explicit constraint checks: one per predecessor, one per resource demanded for each candidate start time, and one per exclusion partner. Each check is *overlooked* with probability ε, and an overlooked check means the constraint is ignored, which can leave the schedule infeasible. The number of checks grows with the size of the affected region, so the probability of at least one harmful slip grows with it. Three error-model families are used. The default is a per-check slip probability ε = 0.02, with ε = 0.005, 0.01 and 0.04 as variations. A *load-dependent* model multiplies ε (0.005) by 1 + 0.5 × region size, representing an agent whose reliability falls as more tasks must be handled at once. A *size-independent* model gives a fixed probability 0.30 of exactly one overlooked check per repair, as a control fixing slip occurrence rather than the probability that a slip causes infeasibility.'))
A(('p', "The second agent audits the first agent's schedule by re-checking every constraint of every region task. Each audit check is itself overlooked with probability ε. Flagged tasks and their descendants in the region are re-placed by the first agent, and the audit is repeated once. Tokens are counted from the checks performed: 300 per repair run, 200 per audit round, 25 per check, 100 per fix round and 150 for an external validator call."))
A(('h2', '3.4. Policies and trigger'))
A(('ul', ['**Always single:** the first agent only.', '**Always team:** the first agent followed by the audit.', '**Dependency trigger:** call the audit if a feature of the constraint difference reaches a threshold θ tuned on the development split. Features are *load* (the number of constraints that must be re-checked in the affected region), *region size* and *number of changed constraints*; all are computed from the constraint difference alone.', '**Random matched:** call the audit with the same overall probability as the trigger, evaluated as an exact expectation.', "**Failure-triggered:** validate the first agent's schedule with an external validator and call the audit only if it fails.", '**Oracle:** call the audit only when the first repair is infeasible, at no validator cost (an unattainable bound).']))
A(('p', 'Policies are compared on a utility that prices tokens against feasibility:'))
A(('eq', dict(key='util', text='U = P(feasible) − λ · tokens')))
A(('p', 'where λ is the feasibility value of one token. The reported λ values are 1/20,000, 1/10,000, 1/5,000 and 1/2,500 per token; the headline value 1/5,000 (1,000 tokens equal 0.2 feasibility) was fixed before looking at test results. A second agent is worth calling on an episode only if the failure probability it removes exceeds its cost:'))
A(('eq', dict(key='rule', text='P(F | x) · Δ_feas > λ · E[tokens_audit | x]')))
A(('h2', '3.5. Statistical analysis'))
A(('p', 'Predictive value is measured by the area under the ROC curve (AUC), computed with the Mann-Whitney statistic. Utilities are reported with 95% cluster-bootstrap intervals that resample base instances (1,000 resamples), because the repetitions and disruptions of one base instance are dependent (Efron, 1979; Field & Welsh, 2007). Differences between the trigger and the random-matched policy are paired by episode. The simulation provides no population, so intervals describe sampling variability across generated instances.'))
A(('h2', '3.6. Ethical approval'))
A(('p', 'The study uses simulated data only and involves no human participants or personal data; ethics approval is not required.'))
A(('h2', '3.7. Primary family and additional structural features'))
A(('p', 'The primary outcome is utility at the inherited headline token price and default per-check slip rate. The revised primary family contains seven paired cluster-level contrasts: each of five feature triggers against rate-matched random invocation, and the load trigger against always-single and always-team. Cluster-level sign-flip tests use 5000 permutations; Holm correction covers all seven contrasts (Holm, 1979). Mean-difference intervals use 2000 cluster resamples and are nominal, not simultaneous. The family is specified for this revision after prior exploratory work and is not retrospectively preregistered. Feasibility, costs, AUC and price-grid maxima are secondary descriptive or exploratory summaries.'))
A(('p', 'Two reviewer-motivated features were fixed before the new run: the fraction of precedence-critical tasks inside the affected region, and duration-weighted resource pressure summed over that region. Criticality uses precedence-only earliest and latest starts and ignores resource/exclusion constraints; it is a proxy, not an exact resource-constrained critical path. AUC intervals resample complete base instances with 500 replicates. Thresholds are selected on development instances and frozen on test instances. A constant-probability slip control fixes the chance of omitting a check, not the chance that an omission causes infeasibility.'))
A(('h2', '3.8. Separate model-driven validation protocol'))
A(('p', 'A separate held-out harness requests scheduling repairs and independent audits from a language model, using public twelve-task constraints, exact domain/feasibility validation and a bounded CP-SAT reference. Development and test streams are disjoint. Cached raw completions, prompt hashes and seed templates are retained. The client exposes a provider-independent interface with a hard per-paper budget cap. Provider discovery found no credentials or ready local model, and the official local-runtime download attempt did not complete. A labelled mock run tests the harness only; no real-model result is inferred.'))
A(('h1', '4. Results'))
A(('h2', '4.1. What the second agent buys'))
rows = []
