d_load = pol[(pol['mode'] == 'eps') & (pol.eps == 0.02) & (pol.lam == '1/5k') & (pol.policy == 'DIFF_ours_vs_random_load')].iloc[0]
A(('p', f"""The tuned trigger on the load feature called the audit for {E.capture(pct(P('ours_load', 'inv')), "build_manuscript.py:132: pct(P('ours_load', 'inv'))", '', -1)} of disruptions, so it was practically equivalent to always-team ({{T:pol}}). At the three other token prices the tuned threshold likewise selected an extreme (never or almost always). The utility difference to a random policy with the same calling rate was {E.capture(d_load.U, 'build_manuscript.py:132: d_load.U', f'.4f', -1)} [{E.capture(d_load.U_lo, 'build_manuscript.py:132: d_load.U_lo', f'.4f', -1)}, {E.capture(d_load.U_hi, 'build_manuscript.py:132: d_load.U_hi', f'.4f', -1)}], which is small; multiplicity-adjusted inference for the fixed primary family is reported separately below. Across a fine grid of 40 token prices, the best gain of the trigger over the better of always-single and always-team was {E.capture(g1.gain_over_best_corner, 'build_manuscript.py:132: g1.gain_over_best_corner', f'.4f', -1)} [{E.capture(g1.gain_lo, 'build_manuscript.py:132: g1.gain_lo', f'.4f', -1)}, {E.capture(g1.gain_hi, 'build_manuscript.py:132: g1.gain_hi', f'.4f', -1)}] at ε = 0.02, {E.capture(g2.gain_over_best_corner, 'build_manuscript.py:132: g2.gain_over_best_corner', f'.4f', -1)} at ε = 0.04, {E.capture(g3.gain_over_best_corner, 'build_manuscript.py:132: g3.gain_over_best_corner', f'.4f', -1)} in the load-dependent condition and {E.capture(g4.gain_over_best_corner, 'build_manuscript.py:132: g4.gain_over_best_corner', f'.4f', -1)} in the size-independent control ({{F:lambda}}). These gains come from skipping the audit for the small share of disruptions with almost no constraints to re-check."""))
A(('p', f"The reason is visible in the second equation. The audit costs {E.capture(tokT - tokA, 'build_manuscript.py:133: tokT - tokA', f'.0f', -1)} tokens and removes about {E.capture(100 * (feasT - feasA), 'build_manuscript.py:133: 100 * (feasT - feasA)', f'.0f', -1)} percentage points of failure on average. To justify the call for a subgroup, that subgroup's failure probability must exceed the break-even level for the token price. For most prices, either every subgroup is above that level (call always) or every subgroup is below it (never call); the features do not separate subgroups widely enough to produce a threshold between the two."))
