A(('h1', '5. Discussion'))
A(('p', f"""The hypothesis that the amount of dependency change after a disruption identifies the repairs that need a second agent is not supported in this testbed. The signal is present, in line with the intuition that larger changes require more checks, but it is weak, and its strength is determined by a property of the agent that the constraint difference cannot observe: whether the agent's reliability declines with load. Where we imposed such a dependence, AUC rose to about {E.capture(A_('region_proxy', 'loaded', 0.005), "build_manuscript.py:154: A_('region_proxy', 'loaded', 0.005)", f'.2f', -1)}, which is useful for ranking but still insufficient to move the optimal policy away from the corners."""))
A(('p', "The negative result is informative for the design of collaboration policies. First, it suggests that the economic decision is dominated by the second agent's average value and cost, and that routing on coarse structural features adds little unless the base error rate is strongly heterogeneous across tasks. This is consistent with reports that coordination benefits depend on task structure and on the capability of the individual agent (Kim et al., 2026), and it complements the selection of topology at the task or delegation boundary (Hong, 2026; Banerjee & Chadha, 2026) by measuring a different signal in a controlled post-disruption testbed; predictive strengths across these tasks are not directly comparable. Second, whenever a cheap validator exists, the rule that calls for help only after a failed validation was better than any structural rule we tried, and the free-signal oracle is an optimistic reference for attainable utility. Third, our results show a practical way to decide: measure the audit's marginal failure removal, compute the break-even failure probability for the token price, and check whether any observable feature separates episodes above and below it."))
A(('p', 'The study design contains the usual risks of simulation work. The error model is mechanistic but assumed: independent overlooked checks. Real agents fail through misreading, hallucinated constraints and loss of context, which may correlate with structure in ways the model does not represent. The load-dependent condition and the cascade disruption were added after the pilot because the first results were small, so they should be treated as exploratory, and the confirmatory claim is limited to the pre-specified conditions.'))
A(('p', 'The absence of real-model validation limits transfer: actual scheduling errors can correlate with semantic ambiguity, context length or shared misconceptions rather than independent skipped checks. The additional structural features test specific alternatives, not the space of all useful routing signals. A real-model result could strengthen the signal and would require changing the conclusion.'))
A(('h1', '6. Conclusion'))
A(('p', f"In a controlled scheduling testbed, a single agent's repair was infeasible in {E.capture(pct(fail_rate), 'build_manuscript.py:159: pct(fail_rate)', '', -1)} of disruptions, a second agent's audit removed most of those failures at {E.capture(tokT / tokA, 'build_manuscript.py:159: tokT / tokA', f'.2f', -1)} times the tokens, and features of the dependency change predicted the failures only weakly. A threshold trigger on those features offered a negligible gain over the better of always-single and always-team, and a failure-triggered rule with a validator was preferable below about {E.capture(cross, 'build_manuscript.py:159: cross', f'.0f', -1)} tokens per validation."))
A(('p', 'RQ1 is answered by the conditional predictive values; RQ2 by paired utility comparisons and the near-corner policies; RQ3 by the validator-cost sensitivity. These answers apply to the simulated error mechanisms and do not establish a general rule against collaboration.'))
A(('h2', '6.1. Recommendations'))
A(('ul', ['Do not assume that structural change signals are sufficient to route collaboration; test the predictive value of the signal against the agent actually used.', "When a validator is available and cheap, prefer failure-triggered collaboration; when it is not, decide between always-single and always-team from the audit's marginal failure removal and the token price.", "Future work should repeat the study with real language-model agents, on REALM-Bench scheduling tasks, and with features that reflect the agent's own uncertainty in addition to the structure of the change."]))
A(('h2', '6.2. Limitations'))
A(('p', 'The agents are algorithmic with injected overlooked checks, not language models, so the quantitative results (failure rates, token counts) do not transfer. Only one task domain with 12-task instances was studied. Utility depends on the token price, which is a modelling choice; we therefore report a grid. The exploratory extensions (cascade, load-dependent errors) were introduced after a pilot, the benchmark REALM-Bench was not used, and the CP-SAT reference is a single solver configuration with one worker and incomplete optimality certification. The new critical-path feature ignores resource-constrained slack and is only a structural proxy. Token counts are assumed charges per check rather than measured model tokens, so their utility is a construct rather than a monetary estimate. Check omissions omit semantic and correlated failure modes. No real-model evaluation was obtained; prompt sensitivity and model-specific generalisation remain unmeasured. Price-grid maxima use test outcomes for descriptive selection and are not independent confirmations. All p-values and intervals concern generated base instances rather than real workloads.'))
